WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.067
The statistics.

2
00:00:01.347 --> 00:00:04.717
Week 3 gave you the tools; here is how they fit the harness.

3
00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:07.621
Compare systems on the same questions, paired.

4
00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:14.728
When you test a change, run the baseline and the candidate on the same cases and analyze the per-question differences.

5
00:00:15.008 --> 00:00:29.600
Evan Miller’s “Adding Error Bars to Evals” (2024) makes the case for paired analysis: questions vary enormously in difficulty, and pairing removes that variation from the comparison, which shrinks the interval a lot.

6
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:38.328
In code: for each question, candidate score minus baseline score, then a bootstrap interval on the mean difference by resampling questions.

7
00:00:38.608 --> 00:00:40.187
Report intervals, always.

8
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:44.349
A mean without an interval invites people to read noise as signal.

9
00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:59.285
Miller also recommends clustered standard errors when questions come in groups (several questions about the same document), because grouped questions are not independent; resample whole groups in your bootstrap if your set has that structure.

10
00:00:59.565 --> 00:01:01.805
Size the set for the effect you care about.

11
00:01:02.085 --> 00:01:05.925
Before you run an experiment, ask what difference would change your decision.

12
00:01:06.205 --> 00:01:11.859
If a 2-point gain in faithfulness wouldn’t change anything, you don’t need the sample size to detect one.

13
00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:23.509
If you need to detect 5 points and your interval half-width at n = 100 is 6 points, either add cases or accept that this eval can only detect large changes.

14
00:01:23.789 --> 00:01:25.197
Say which in your write-up.

15
00:01:25.477 --> 00:01:27.077
Handle non-determinism.

16
00:01:27.357 --> 00:01:31.368
If outputs vary between runs, one run per question adds noise.

17
00:01:31.648 --> 00:01:39.392
Either run each question a few times and average per question, or at least rerun the baseline to see how much it moves on its own.

18
00:01:39.672 --> 00:01:43.811
A “gain” smaller than the baseline’s run-to-run movement is not a gain.

19
00:01:44.091 --> 00:01:46.117
Decide your stopping rule in advance.

20
00:01:46.397 --> 00:01:52.328
Running the eval repeatedly and stopping when the number looks good is a way to manufacture a result.

21
00:01:52.608 --> 00:01:56.000
Fix the dataset version and the comparison before you look.
